exams a five-letter word which most of  us wished didn't exist but sadly  the truth is that exams are a vital part  of our lives  to progress further we all need to face  them  and ace them exams cause distress to  many  but exams are important they teach us  discipline  make us prepared for facing potential  situations  and also test our proficiency which on  the longer run  makes us a better person looking back at  my childhood and adulthood  i was fond of a lot of things playing  with my friends  sharing lunch sports traveling and of  course  biology but what i was not particularly  fond of  was exams now at this stage i see  a lot of people struggle with exams  they struggle not because they haven't  studied enough or due to a lack of  knowledge  but they struggle due to certain factors  which i plan to talk about in this video  hi my name is dr umar malik and this is  med simplified  let's talk about a few scientific ways  that will improve your exam preparation  and help you ace any exam you may face  number one planning most of the students  including me make this one simple  yet a very important thing we often  don't plan for how to prepare for an  exam  we just tell ourselves that we will  study as much as possible  and then hope for the best this isn't a  wise approach  preparing for an exam is like taking on  an important project  take for example building a bridge would  you start building a bridge without  first coming up with a plan  would you just begin construction and  hope for the best  no you wouldn't in a similar way it's  important to outline  in writing a strategy for how you'll get  ready for an exam  according to many scientific studies  brain adjusts to patterns  and predetermined tasks faster and also  more efficiently  rather than going about half as early so  keeping that in mind  here are some questions you should  consider as a part of your plan  when will you start studying how many  hours each day  will you dedicate to studying which  topics do you need further clarification  on  which homework assignments will you  review how many practice exams will you  do  how many times will you review your  notes and your textbook readings  what distractions are you likely to face  and how will you overcome them  as the saying goes failing to plan is  planning to fail  so make sure you have a plan for every  exam you take  draw up a list follow it religiously and  watch your performance boost  number two get on a sleep schedule at  least one week before the exam  many exams are scheduled for early in  the morning  a time when students don't usually feel  very awake  so it's always a better habit to start  sleeping earlier  at least one week before the exam so  that your body will have time to adjust  to this new schedule  if not your mind won't be in an optimal  state to take the exam  when you are sitting there giving the  exam you need your mind to be  on its peak performance so always ensure  you get  eight hours of sleep each night getting  enough sleep  is vital if you want to improve your  memory and mental focus  neuro studies have shown that when you  sleep the brain processes various  informations  from the previous day filtering out  important memories and consolidating the  information  it's tempting to stay up late or even  pull up  a few all-nighters in the days and weeks  leading up to the exam  but this is counterproductive you will  do much better on an exam if you get  adequate rest  to know how to sleep better and do's and  don'ts of sleep hygiene  watch our video on this topic by  clicking the banner above  number three don't do any last minute  studying  right before the exam it's common for  students to spend the last 10 minutes  before an exam  doing some last minute studying this may  provide some psychological comfort  but overall it does more harm than good  10 minutes before an exam you either  know the required information or you  don't  if you frantically review facts or  equations during this time  you will become more stressed and  anxious this will  negatively affect your performance  instead  make use of the time before the start of  the exam to relax  visualize yourself answering the  question correctly and imagine yourself  in a state of peace  and confidence as a college student i  always had a trick to calm myself down  before an exam  take a deep breath inhale for 4 seconds  and then exhale for 4 seconds repeat  this  as many times as necessary to calm down  trust me it will help a calm mind does  wonders  number four turn your focus towards the  process  and away from the outcome take a look at  professional athletes of  nba one second on the clock all the  pressure and cameras and  eyes on them imagine how intense it  becomes  at that moment when a player scores a  game-winning shot  when journalists ask them what was going  through your mind when you took that  shot  you might expect them to say something  like i thought about how the crowd would  react if i missed the shot  or i focused on how much time was left  in the game  but i've always observed that they  rarely say  anything like that their most common  reply is something like this  i just took the shot the same way i have  taken it thousands of times before in  the practice  it's this focus on the process and trust  on their hard work and practice  rather than the focus on the outcome  that allows professional athletes to  achieve  better outcomes this means that if you  focus  primarily on the outcomes for instance  repeatedly thinking to yourself  i must ace this exam i must ace this  exam  you're probably going to stress yourself  out and perform waivers  if you want to ace the exam you should  instead concentrate on staying calm  on thinking through each question  carefully and on analyzing  what each question is really asking  number five let's talk about some useful  exam taking techniques that you can  implement for better grades  students like to think that exams are an  objective measure of learning  but they're really not you might know  the information  but if you're not equipped with the  necessary exam taking skills  you won't get the grades you're hoping  for my seniors and elders taught this to  me  and i hope this helps you here are some  basic  exam taking principles and techniques  be familiar with the structure of the  exam it's important to know the answers  to these questions  how many sections will the exam have  what are the differences between the  sections  will there be multiple choice questions  open-ended questions  or both how many points will there be in  the total  use a stopwatch during an exam every  second counts  using a stopwatch will help you keep  track of the time  more accurately use a stopwatch during  your exam preparations as well  it will help you micromanage your time  and skills the inner clock  look through the entire exam at the  beginning when the exam starts  don't jump straight in and start  answering the questions  instead take a minute or two to skim all  of the questions  get a feel for how challenging the exam  is overall  and take a note of which questions will  be especially time consuming  set aside more time to tackle those  questions  this will avoid any surprises you may  feel as you go deeper  into the exam know how much time  you should spend on each question based  on how long the exam is  and how many points there are in total  you'll know roughly  how much time to spend on each question  for example  if you have 50 minutes to complete a 50  point exam  you have one minute per point so  for a 10 point question you should spend  approximately 10 minutes answering it  if after 10 minutes you're still not  done with the question  it's time to speed up if you get stuck  move on if you are unable to solve a  problem  maintain your composure and go on to the  next one  time is always ticking when you have  completed the rest of the exam  come back to the unfinished problems  that way  even if one part doesn't work out for  you you can utilize time to the past you  know  and can easily score from with that we  come to the end of this  short video friends i want all of you to  work hard and succeed in life  i hope this video helps you in that  process  make sure to hit the like button below  and make sure to share this video with  your friends  subscribe to our channel if you're new  we at mess simplified are committed to  bringing you  new quality content every week support  us by becoming a member of our channel  on youtube or patreon.com slash med  simplified  also you can connect to us through  instagram where we post something new  and fun  every day links are in the description  below  thank you so much for watching and stay  safe see you  in the next video
 

